## Why This Is Asked
Tests an engineer's ability to design incremental statistics with tight per-batch updates and constant-time queries, a realistic analytics constraint.

## Key Concepts
- Maintaining a nonlinear statistic (entropy) via a linearizable surrogate S
- Precomputing k log k and handling zero edges cleanly
- Incremental updates using two counters per move

## Code Example
```javascript
// G[k] = k * Math.log(k), with G[0] = 0
function processBatch(batch, freq, G, S, n) {
  for (const {from,to} of batch) {
    const fFrom = freq[from];
    freq[from] = fFrom - 1;
    S.value += G[fFrom - 1] - G[fFrom];
    const fTo = freq[to];
    freq[to] = fTo + 1;
    S.value += G[fTo + 1] - G[fTo];
  }
  return Math.log(n) - S.value / n;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if m changes over time or updates include new categories?
- How do you handle numerical stability for very large n and skewed distributions?

## Why This Is Asked
New angle on complexity with top-k heavy hitters in streaming data, bounded memory, and batch updates. Tests ability to reason about error guarantees and practical trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming top-k with bounded memory
- Space-Saving / Frequent Items algorithms
- Per-element vs batch cost
- Error guarantees: ε ≤ total_count/k
- Trade-offs vs exact O(M) memory

## Code Example
```javascript
class SpaceSaving {
  constructor(k){ this.k=k; this.map=new Map(); this.counterHeap=[]; }
  update(x){ if(this.map.has(x)){ this.map.get(x).cnt++; return; }
    if(this.map.size < this.k){ this.map.set(x,{cnt:1}); return; }
    // replace min counter item
    let minKey = null, minCnt = Infinity;
    for(const [key,v] of this.map){ if(v.cnt < minCnt){ minCnt=v.cnt; minKey=key; } }
    this.map.delete(minKey);
    this.map.set(x,{cnt:minCnt+1});
  }
  topK(){ // shallow: return current keys with counts
    return Array.from(this.map.entries()).sort((a,b)=>b[1].cnt-a[1].cnt).map(([k,v])=>({key:k,count:v.cnt}));
  }
}
```

