## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of streaming data structures, heap-based algorithms, and amortized complexity analysis in a real-time processing context.

## Key Concepts
- **Two-heap streaming median:** A max-heap stores the lower half; a min-heap stores the upper half.
- **Rebalancing invariant:** Heap sizes differ by at most one, ensuring the median is accessible in O(1).
- **Time complexity:** O(log n) per element for insertion plus O(log n) for rebalancing.
- **Space complexity:** O(n), proportional to total elements seen.

## Code Example
```javascript
insert(x) {
  if (L.isEmpty() || x <= L.max()) {
    L.insert(x);
  } else {
    R.insert(x);
  }
  rebalance();
}

rebalance() {
  if (L.size() > R.size() + 1) {
    R.insert(L.extractMax());
  } else if (R.size() > L.size() + 1) {
    L.insert(R.extractMin());
  }
}

getMedian() {
  if (L.size() > R.size()) return L.max();
  if (R.size() > L.size()) return R.min();
  return (L.max() + R.min()) / 2;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of streaming algorithms for distinct counting with bounded memory, including practical trade-offs between accuracy and resources.

## Key Concepts
- **HyperLogLog sketches**: Probabilistic data structure using leading-zero counting to estimate cardinalities
- **Memory-accuracy trade-off**: With m registers, expected relative error is ~1.04/√m (e.g., 13% error with 64 registers)
- **Per-element update**: O(1) time - hash, extract bucket, compute rank, update register
- **Batch processing**: Process each element in the batch sequentially, output estimate after all updates
- **Comparison to exact counting**: Exact counting requires O(N) memory to store all seen values (or at minimum a bitmap of size M), while HLL uses constant O(m) memory regardless of N

## Code Example
```javascript
class HyperLogLog {
  constructor(m = 64) {
    this.m = m;
    this.registers = new Uint8Array(m);
  }

  update(x) {
    const h = this.hash64(x);
    const bucketBits = Math.log2(this.m);
    const bucket = h >> (64 - bucketBits);
    const rank = this.leadingZeros((h << bucketBits) | 1) + 1;
    this.registers[bucket] = Math.max(this.registers[bucket], rank);
  }

  estimate() {
    const sum = this.registers.reduce((acc, r) => acc + Math.pow(2, -r), 0);
    return (this.m * this.m) / sum;
  }

  hash64(x) { /* MurmurHash3 or similar */ }
  leadingZeros(x) { /* Count leading zeros in x */ }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of streaming cardinality estimation, space-time tradeoffs, and practical parallelism. HyperLogLog is a standard tool; candidates should justify pick of m, how to bound δ, and merge semantics in multi-process pipelines.

## Key Concepts

- HyperLogLog accuracy: error ~1.04/√m
- Independence via multiple seeds to bound δ
- Merging: element-wise max across registers
- Time/space: O(B) updates, O(m) memory

## Code Example

```javascript
function updateHLL(hll, x) {
  const r = hash32(x);
  const idx = r >>> (32 - p);
  const w = (r << p) | (/* extra bits for rank */ 0);
  hll.registers[idx] = Math.max(hll.registers[idx], rank(w));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How does batch size B influence constants in practice?
- How would you implement cross-shard merging to obtain a global estimate?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests understanding of dynamic connectivity under incremental edge insertions and practical amortized analysis in graph algorithms.

## Key Concepts

- Disjoint Set Union (DSU) with path compression and union by size
- Amortized α(n) (inverse Ackermann function) for near-constant operations
- Batch processing efficiency vs full recomputation approaches

## Code Example

```javascript
class DSU {
  constructor(n) {
    this.parent = Array.from({length: n}, (_, i) => i);
    this.size = new Array(n).fill(1);
    this.components = n;
  }

  find(a) {
    if (this.parent[a] !== a) {
      this.parent[a] = this.find(this.parent[a]);
    }
    return this.parent[a];
  }

  union(a, b) {
    const rootA = this.find(a);
    const rootB = this.find(b);
    if (rootA === rootB) return false;

    if (this.size[rootA] < this.size[rootB]) {
      [rootA, rootB] = [rootB, rootA];
    }
    this.parent[rootB] = rootA;
    this.size[rootA] += this.size[rootB];
    this.components--;
    return true;
  }
}

function processBatches(n, batches) {
  const dsu = new DSU(n);
  const results = [];

  for (const batch of batches) {
    for (const [u, v] of batch) {
      dsu.union(u, v);
    }
    results.push(dsu.components);
  }

  return results;
}
```

## Analysis

**Preprocessing:** O(n) time to initialize DSU arrays, O(n) memory for parent and size arrays.

**Per-batch updates:** O(B·α(n)) time for B union operations with path compression and union by size.

**Total complexity:** O(n + m·α(n)) time, O(n) memory.

**Comparison to recomputation:** Full DFS/BFS after each batch would cost O(n + m) per batch, totaling O((n + m)·(m/B)) time—significantly worse for large graphs with many small batches.

The DSU approach leverages amortized near-constant operations, making it ideal for dynamic connectivity scenarios with incremental updates.

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design streaming window data structures and perform amortized analysis under batched inputs, a common real-world challenge.

## Key Concepts

- Sliding window with evictions
- Top-k maintenance with exact counts
- Lazy cleanup in a heap to bound update cost
- Amortized analysis in batch-input settings
- Trade-offs vs recomputing from scratch

## Code Example

```javascript
// Sketch of a TopKWindow with exact counts and lazy heap maintenance
class TopKWindow {
  constructor(K, W) {
    this.K = K; this.W = W;
    this.counts = new Map();
    this.window = [];
    // Minimal placeholder for a max-heap with push/peek/pop
    this.heap = new MaxHeap((a,b) => b.count - a.count);
  }
  insert(id) {
    this.window.push(id);
    const c = (this.counts.get(id) || 0) + 1;
    this.counts.set(id, c);
    this.heap.push({id, count: c});
    if (this.window.length > this.W) this.evict();
  }
  evict() {
    const id = this.window.shift();
    const c = (this.counts.get(id) || 0) - 1;
    this.counts.set(id, c);
    this.heap.push({id, count: c}); // lazy
  }
  topK() {
    // prune stale entries
    const out = [];
    while (out.length < this.K) {
      const top = this.heap.peek();
      if (!top) break;
      const real = this.counts.get(top.id) || 0;
      if (top.count !== real) {
        this.heap.pop();
        continue;
      }
      out.push(top.id);
      this.heap.pop();
    }
    return out;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle ties in top-3?
- How does changing W or B affect space/time, and can you adapt to varying K?

